{"qid":"Q1709007","names":["Portuguese Ceylon"],"title":"Portuguese Ceylon","endonym":null,"capsule":"Portuguese Ceylon denotes the coastal and lowland territories of Sri Lanka that Portugal controlled from its first landing at Colombo in 1505 through successive military campaigns, eventually establishing dominance over the Kotte kingdom by 1597 when its last king, Dharmapala, bequeathed his realm to the Portuguese crown. Portugal never controlled the interior Kandyan kingdom, whose rulers repeatedly repelled Portuguese expeditions, but held the maritime cinnamon-producing littoral as a commercially vital outpost connecting its Estado da India network. The Portuguese were expelled by the Dutch VOC between 1638 and 1658 through a series of sieges conducted in alliance with Kandy; the final Portuguese fortress at Jaffna fell in 1658, though Dutch sovereignty was not fully consolidated until the 1660s.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Coastal and fortress extents are well documented in Portuguese and Dutch records; the hinterland boundary with Kandy was contested and fluid throughout this period.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Portuguese Ceylon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_Ceylon"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kotte kingdom","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotte_kingdom"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dutch Ceylon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Ceylon"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Portuguese Ceylon","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Pedro Lopes de Sousa","nativeName":"Pedro Lopes de Sousa","roman":"Pedro Lopes de Sousa","start":1594,"end":1594,"image":nul